      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The purpose of this article was to assess the impact of intravenous (IV) anticholinergic hyoscine butylbromide in abdominal magnetic resonance (MR) imaging with oral magnetic particles (OMP) [ABDOS-CAN, Ferristene (USAN), Nycomed Imaging AS, Oslo, Norway]."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "In 31 patients with abdominal tumors, T1-weighted spin-echo (SE) images (SE 600/ 15; 1.5 T) were obtained without and with IV hyoscine butylbromide (20 mg) before and after administration of 800 ml of OMP. Two blinded readers assessed motion artifacts, bowel-wall visualization, and lesion delineation on the four sets of T1-weighted images. The two-tailed Wilcoxon paired sample test was used for statistical analysis (p < .05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Hyoscine butylbromide reduced motion artifacts and improved bowel-wall visualization on precontrast and OMP-enhanced images at a statistically significant level (p = 0.0006-0.037). The lowest degree of artifacts was recorded on OMP images with hyoscine butylbromide. OMP with hyoscine butylbromide significantly improved lesion delineation compared to studies without antiperistaltic drug before (p = 0.019) and after OMP administration (p = 0.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The authors conclude that the use of IV hyoscine butylbromide is recommended for OMP-enhanced abdominal MR imaging with T1-weighted SE pulse sequences at 1.5 T."
        }
      ]},
